                          PASCALINE TO VERILOG CONVERTION

The idea of the Pascaline to Verilog convertion process is that the code is
translated to a state machine. Each process is its own state machine, and the
main program and all of its routines are such state machines.

The upshot of that is that a program, even one spread across modules, must be
compacted into a single code block by processing for inline code.

Each node of the code becomes a state, and the expressions trail off each state.
Although verilog has the ability to process loops, we instead create states for
each node in the loop, and let the state machine run the loop.

With the flow of control subtracted, and all of the procedure and function calls
flattened, what is left is truly rtl, or a series of assignments. The data 
mapping for the program becomes important. Each variable is mapped, each array
and structure, and the operations on those performed as formulas.
